@node Naming system
@section Naming System

Lingo uses @code{case-insensitive} naming with identifiers normalized to
@code{Header-Case} (Title-Case-With-Hyphens). The lexer converts camelCase
and PascalCase identifiers to Header-Case format.

@example
myVariable   @result{} My-Variable
playerHealth @result{} Player-Health
SPRITE_COUNT @result{} Sprite-Count
@end example

@table @asis
@item Keywords
@code{if}, @code{then}, @code{else}, @code{end if}, @code{repeat},
@code{with}, @code{for}, @code{to}, @code{step}, @code{end repeat},
@code{while}, @code{until}, @code{do}, @code{loop}, @code{exit},
@code{next}, @code{on}, @code{end}, @code{property}, @code{global},
@code{set}, @code{get}, @code{put}, @code{putprop}, @code{getprop},
@code{call}, @code{goto}, @code{return}, @code{print}, @code{input},
@code{of}, @code{the}, @code{and}, @code{or}, @code{not},
@code{true}, @code{false}, @code{null}, @code{self}

@item Operators
@code{+}, @code{-}, @code{*}, @code{/}, @code{=}, @code{<>}, @code{!=},
@code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=}, @code{and}, @code{or}, @code{not}

@item Number Formats
Decimal (@code{123}), hexadecimal (@code{0xFF} or @code{$FF} or @code{$hFF}),
octal (@code{0o77} or @code{$o77}), binary (@code{0b1010} or @code{%1010} or @code{$b1010})

@item String Delimiters
Double quotes (@code{"..."}) or single quotes (@code{'...'})

@item Comments
Two hyphens to end of line (@code{-- comment}), or @code{(* block comment *)}
@end table

@node Method and procedure definitions
@section Method and Procedure Definitions

@findex on
@findex end
@findex procedure
@findex method

Methods and procedures define named blocks of code that can be called
from other handlers or events.

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

A method or procedure is a named subroutine that encapsulates a sequence
of statements. Methods are associated with objects (parent scripts), while
procedures are standalone routines.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
on HandlerName
    @var{statements}
end

on HandlerName param1, param2
    @var{statements}
end

procedure ProcedureName
    @var{statements}
end

procedure ProcedureName param1, param2
    @var{statements}
end
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
on initializeGame
    gScore = 0
    gLivesLeft = 3
    gCurrentRoom = 1
end

on calculateBonus level, timeLeft
    return level * 100 + timeLeft * 10
end

procedure updateDisplay
    put gScore
    put gLivesLeft
end
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item Zero or more parameter names separated by commas
@item Parameters are passed by value (copy)
@item Variable number of arguments not supported
@end itemize

@subsubheading Return Values

Use @code{return expression} to return a value to the caller.
Without an expression, returns void.

@node Property and global declarations
@section Property and Global Declarations

@findex property
@findex global

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

@code{property} declares instance variables for parent scripts (objects),
while @code{global} declares variables that persist across handlers and
maintain state throughout script execution.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
property prop1, prop2, prop3

global gVar1, gVar2
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Parent script: Player
property pX
property pY
property pHealth
property pSprite

on new me, x, y, sprite
    me.pX = x
    me.pY = y
    me.pHealth = 100
    me.pSprite = sprite
    return me
end

-- In another handler
global gHighScore
on saveGame
    global gHighScore
    if gScore > gHighScore then
        gHighScore = gScore
    end if
end
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Property names: any valid identifier
@item Global names: any valid identifier, conventionally prefixed with @code{g}
@item Must be declared before use within a handler
@end itemize

@subsubheading Outputs

@code{property} creates instance variables accessible via @code{me.propertyName}.
@code{global} makes variables accessible across handler boundaries.

@node Conditional execution
@section Conditional Execution

@findex if
@findex then
@findex else
@findex end if

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Conditional statements execute different code paths based on boolean
expressions. Lingo supports single if/then, if/then/else, and chained
if/then/else if/else structures.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
if @var{expression} then
    @var{statements}
end if

if @var{expression} then
    @var{statements}
else
    @var{statements}
end if

if @var{expression1} then
    @var{statements1}
else if @var{expression2} then
    @var{statements2}
else
    @var{statements3}
end if
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
if gScore > 1000 then
    put "High score!"
end if

if gHealth <= 0 then
    gameOver = TRUE
    go to "GameOver"
else
    gHealth = gHealth - damage
end if

if temperature > 30 then
    status = "HOT"
else if temperature > 15 then
    status = "WARM"
else if temperature > 0 then
    status = "COOL"
else
    status = "COLD"
end if
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Any expression that evaluates to TRUE or FALSE
@item Use @code{and}, @code{or}, @code{not} for complex conditions
@item Comparison operators: @code{=}, @code{<>}, @code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=}
@end itemize

@subsubheading Outputs

Boolean TRUE/FALSE determines which branch executes.

@subsubheading Case Statement

Lingo also supports multi-way branching via @code{case}:

@example
case expression of
    value1, value2:
        @var{statements}
    value3:
        @var{statements}
    otherwise:
        @var{statements}
end case
@end example

@node Loop constructs
@section Loop Constructs

@findex repeat
@findex with
@findex for
@findex in
@findex to
@findex step
@findex while
@findex until
@findex end repeat
@findex exit
@findex next

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Loops repeat a block of statements either a fixed number of times,
while a condition holds, or until a condition becomes true.

@subsubheading Numeric Loop (FOR)

@example
repeat with counter = @var{start} to @var{end}
    @var{statements}
end repeat

repeat with counter = @var{start} to @var{end} step @var{step}
    @var{statements}
end repeat
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Count 1 to 10
repeat with i = 1 to 10
    put "Count:" && i
end repeat

-- Count 0 to 100 by 5s
repeat with i = 0 to 100 step 5
    processValue(i)
end repeat

-- Count down from 10 to 1
repeat with i = 10 down to 1
    put "T-minus" && i
end repeat
@end example

@subsubheading While Loop (PRE-TEST)

@example
repeat while @var{condition}
    @var{statements}
end repeat
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
i = 1
repeat while i <= 100
    if item i of gArray = target then
        put "Found at" && i
        exit repeat
    end if
    i = i + 1
end repeat
@end example

@subsubheading Until Loop (POST-TEST)

@example
repeat
    @var{statements}
until @var{condition}
end repeat
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
repeat
    getInput()
    updateGame()
until gQuitFlag = TRUE
@end example

@subsubheading Repeat Forever

@example
repeat forever
    @var{statements}
    if quitCondition then
        exit repeat
    end if
end repeat
@end example

@subsubheading Loop Control

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{exit repeat} — Exit the innermost loop immediately
@item @code{next repeat} — Skip to next iteration of loop
@end itemize

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Counter variable: any valid identifier
@item Start/end values: numeric expressions
@item Step value: numeric expression (default 1)
@end itemize

@node Input and output
@section Input and Output

@findex put
@findex get
@findex input
@findex print

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

I/O statements handle user interaction, displaying output, and reading
input values.

@subsubheading PUT Statement

@example
put @var{expression}
put @var{expression} to @var{destination}
put @var{string} && @var{variable}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
put "Hello, World!"
put gScore
put "The score is:" && gScore
put myList
put "Result:" && (x + y) to field "output"
@end example

@subsubheading GET Statement

@example
get @var{variable}
@end example

Reads a line of input into the specified variable.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
get gUserInput
get playerName
@end example

@subsubheading INPUT Statement

@example
input @var{variable}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
input userName
input gScoreValue
@end example

@subsubheading PRINT Statement

@example
print @var{expression}
print @var{expression}, @var{expression}, ...
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
print "Starting game"
print "Score:", gScore, "Lives:", gLivesLeft
print result * multiplier
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Output expressions: strings, numbers, variables, or any valid expression
@item Input variables: must be valid identifiers
@item String concatenation: use @code{&&} operator
@end itemize

@node Assignment statements
@section Assignment Statements

@findex set
@findex move
@findex to

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Assignment statements store values into variables, object properties,
or array elements.

@subsubheading Simple Assignment

@example
set @var{variable} = @var{expression}
@var{variable} = @var{expression}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
set gScore = 0
gLivesLeft = 3
gPlayerName = "Hero"
set counter = counter + 1
@end example

@subsubheading Move Statement

@example
move @var{expression} to @var{variable}
@end example

Copies the value of expression to variable.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
move 100 to gScore
move gTemp to gFinal
move (x * 2) to result
@end example

@subsubheading Object Property Assignment

@example
set the @var{property} of @var{object} = @var{value}
@var{object}.@var{property} = @var{value}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
set the pX of player = 100
player.pY = 200
set the visible of sprite 5 to TRUE
@end example

@subsubheading Array/List Assignment

@example
set item @var{index} of @var{list} = @var{value}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
set item 1 of scores = 100
set item i of gameBoard = EMPTY
list[3] = newValue
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Variable names: any valid identifier
@item Property names: any valid identifier
@item Index: positive integer (1-based)
@item Value: any valid expression
@end itemize

@node Routine calls
@section Routine Calls

@findex call
@findex invoke
@findex return

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Routine calls invoke handlers or methods, optionally passing arguments
and receiving return values.

@subsubheading Nullary Call

@example
@var{handler}()
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
initializeGame()
clearScreen()
playStartupMusic()
@end example

@subsubheading Call with Arguments

@example
@var{handler}(arg1, arg2, arg3)
call("@var{handler}"(arg1, arg2))
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
drawSprite(spriteId, x, y)
loadLevel(levelNumber)
calculateScore(playerId, timeBonus)
@end example

@subsubheading Library Calls

@example
call "@var{routine}" of libraryName
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
call "loadGraphics" of "GraphicsLib"
call "PlaySound" of gSoundEngine
@end example

@subsubheading Method Invocation

@example
@var{object}:#@var{method}()
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
player:#move(5, 10)
enemy:#takeDamage(50)
ui:#updateDisplay()
@end example

@subsubheading Return Values

@example
return
return @var{expression}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
return
return gScore
return level * 100 + bonus
@end example

@node Control flow
@section Control Flow

@findex goto
@findex go
@findex play
@findex stop

@subsubheading GOTO Statement

@example
goto @var{label}
go to @var{label}
@end example

Transfers execution to the specified label within the same handler.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
goto skipSection
if error then
    goto errorHandler
end if
@end example

@subsubheading GO Statement

@example
go frame "frameName"
go to @var{label}
@end example

Transitions to another frame or label.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
go frame "Title"
go to "MainLoop"
@end example

@subsubheading PLAY Statement

@example
play frame "frameName"
@end example

Starts playback at specified frame.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
play frame "Intro"
play frame "Credits"
@end example

@subsubheading STOP Statement

@example
stop
stop loop
@end example

Halts movie playback or current loop.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
if gQuitFlag then
    stop
end if
@end example

@node Arithmetic operations
@section Arithmetic Operations

@findex +
@findex -
@findex *
@findex /
@findex ^

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Arithmetic operations perform numeric calculations on operands.

@subsubheading Addition

@example
result = @var{a} + @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
gTotal = gPrice + gTax
gScore = gScore + 10
gX = leftMargin + offset
@end example

@subsubheading Subtraction

@example
result = @var{a} - @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
gBalance = gTotal - gDeductions
gHealth = gHealth - damage
gX = gX - moveSpeed
@end example

@subsubheading Multiplication

@example
result = @var{a} * @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
gArea = width * height
gTotal = price * quantity
gInterest = principal * rate * years
@end example

@subsubheading Division

@example
result = @var{a} / @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
gAverage = gSum / count
gSpeed = distance / time
gRatio = width / height
@end example

@subsubheading Exponentiation

@example
result = @var{a} ^ @var{b}
@end example

Raises @var{a} to the power of @var{b}.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
gSquared = x ^ 2
gVolume = length ^ 3
gDistance = ((x2 - x1) ^ 2 + (y2 - y1) ^ 2) ^ 0.5
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item All operands: numeric values
@item Division by zero: results in error
@item Overflow: implementation-dependent
@end itemize

@subsubheading Bit Operations

Lingo provides bitwise functions for shift operations:

@example
gResult = bitShift(gValue, 2)    -- left shift 2 bits (multiply by 4)
gResult = bitShift(gValue, -1)   -- right shift 1 bit (divide by 2)
@end example

@node Boolean expressions
@section Boolean Expressions

@findex and
@findex or
@findex not
@findex =
@findex <>
@findex <
@findex >
@findex <=
@findex >=

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Boolean expressions evaluate conditions and combine multiple tests.

@subsubheading Equality

@example
@var{a} = @var{b}
@end example

True if @var{a} equals @var{b}.

@subsubheading Inequality

@example
@var{a} <> @var{b}
@var{a} != @var{b}
@end example

True if @var{a} does not equal @var{b}.

@subsubheading Less Than

@example
@var{a} < @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Greater Than

@example
@var{a} > @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Less Than or Equal

@example
@var{a} <= @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Greater Than or Equal

@example
@var{a} >= @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Logical AND

@example
@var{a} and @var{b}
@end example

True if both @var{a} and @var{b} are true.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
if (gScore > 100) and (lives > 0) then
    continueGame()
end if
@end example

@subsubheading Logical OR

@example
@var{a} or @var{b}
@end example

True if either @var{a} or @var{b} (or both) are true.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
if (choice = "Y") or (choice = "y") then
    proceed()
end if
@end example

@subsubheading Logical NOT

@example
not @var{expression}
@end example

Inverts the boolean value.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
if not gameOver then
    updateGame()
end if

if not (x < min or x > max) then
    inRange = TRUE
end if
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Comparison operands: compatible types
@item Logical operators: boolean values
@item Short-circuit evaluation: supported
@end itemize

@subsubheading Operator Precedence

Highest to lowest:
@code{not}, @code{*}, @code{/}, @code{+}, @code{-},
@code{=}, @code{<>}, @code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=},
@code{and}, @code{or}

@node Unsupported features
@section Unsupported Features

The following Lingo features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O
open, read, write, close, append, deleteFile, etc.

@item Graphics primitives
drawRect, drawOval, drawLine, drawPoly, etc.

@item Hardware access
joystick, gameControllers, extended mouse features

@item Multimedia
sound, movie, MIDI, QuickTime-specific commands

@item Extended memory
manual memory allocation, pointers, pointer arithmetic

@item Exception handling
try, catch, finally, throw

@item Preprocessor
#define, #ifdef, #ifndef, #endif, #macro

@item 3D features
3D scene manipulation, model commands

@item Network
netLingo, URL access, socket operations

@item COM/ActiveX
ActiveX control manipulation

@item Extended syntax
list comprehensions, lambda expressions, table operations beyond basic
@end table

